---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

const headline =
  'Plumstead designer launches free app showing Greenwich residents who represents them';
const title = `${headline}. Press release`;
const description =
  'My Greenwich turns a Royal Borough of Greenwich postcode into your ward, your councillors and your MP, with a free directory of independent local businesses. Free on the App Store.';

const site = 'https://theinteractivedesigner.co.uk';
const canonical = `${site}/press/my-greenwich-launch/`;
const appStore = 'https://apps.apple.com/gb/app/my-greenwich/id6803486290';
const pressEmail = 'contact@mygreenwich.uk';
const published = '2026-11-17';

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'NewsArticle',
  '@id': `${canonical}#release`,
  headline,
  description,
  datePublished: published,
  dateModified: published,
  inLanguage: 'en-GB',
  mainEntityOfPage: canonical,
  url: canonical,
  author: { '@id': `${site}/#philip` },
  publisher: { '@id': `${site}/#svpply` },
  about: {
    '@type': 'SoftwareApplication',
    name: 'My Greenwich',
    operatingSystem: 'iOS',
    url: 'https://mygreenwich.uk',
    installUrl: appStore,
  },
};

const facts = [
  ['Name', 'My Greenwich'],
  ['What it does', "Shows a resident's ward, ward councillors and MP from a postcode, plus independent local businesses nearby."],
  ['Area', 'Royal Borough of Greenwich: 23 wards, 55 councillors.'],
  ['Released', 'November 2026'],
  ['Price', 'Free. No account, no advertising.'],
  ['Platform', 'iPhone, iOS 26 or later. Not yet on Android.'],
  ['Listings', 'Free for independent businesses based in the borough. Optional My Greenwich+ upgrade, £4.99 for 12 months with no auto-renewal, adds a photo and a labelled Featured slot.'],
  ['Data', "Office for National Statistics postcode data via postcodes.io, the Royal Borough of Greenwich's published councillor information, and UK Parliament."],
  ['Made by', 'Philip Brumpton, SVPPLY&.CO LIMITED, Plumstead SE18.'],
];
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} type="article" published={published}>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} is:inline />

  <article class="cv press">
    <header>
      <p class="role-meta">
        <a href="/press/">Press</a>. For immediate release,
        <time datetime={published}>17 November 2026</time>
      </p>
      <h1>{headline}</h1>
      <p class="standfirst">
        My Greenwich turns a postcode into your ward, your councillors and your MP, with a
        free directory of independent local businesses.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section id="release">
      <p>
        A free iPhone app that tells residents of the Royal Borough of Greenwich who
        represents them is now available on the App Store. My Greenwich was designed and
        built by Philip Brumpton, a product designer who lives in Plumstead, independently
        of the council.
      </p>
      <p>
        Enter a Greenwich postcode and the app shows which of the borough's 23 wards it is
        in, the councillors for that ward with their party and how to contact them, and the
        MP for the constituency. Greenwich has 55 councillors, two or three for each ward.
        Everything links back to the council's and Parliament's own pages.
      </p>
      <blockquote>
        <p>
          "Most people I talk to locally couldn't tell you which ward they live in, let
          alone who their councillors are. The information is all public, it's just spread
          across several websites. I wanted one screen that answers the question and then
          gets out of the way."
        </p>
        <p class="role-meta">Philip Brumpton, designer of My Greenwich</p>
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        The app is neutral by design. Councillors are shown in the same way whichever party
        they represent, with no commentary and no ratings. There is no sign-up, no
        advertising and no tracking, and the postcode a resident enters stays on their
        phone.
      </p>
      <p>
        My Greenwich also includes a directory of independent businesses, sole traders and
        community groups across the borough, sorted by category and shown nearest first. A
        basic listing is free and every submission is checked by hand before it appears.
        Chains and franchises are not listed.
      </p>
      <blockquote>
        <p>
          "The directory is free to join and sorted by distance, so the cafe or trader
          nearest to you comes first. It's a small way of putting independent businesses in
          front of the people who live round the corner from them."
        </p>
        <p class="role-meta">Philip Brumpton</p>
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        The app covers all 23 wards, from Thamesmead Moorings to Eltham Page. It was built
        to the accessibility standards expected of local government services, with support
        for larger text, VoiceOver and reduced motion.
      </p>
      <p>
        My Greenwich is the third civic app from Brumpton, after
        <a href="https://whoismymp.uk">Who Is My MP?</a> and
        <a href="https://thelords.app">The Lords</a>, which use Parliament's public data. He
        plans to bring the same approach to other London boroughs, and is open to working
        with councils that want to manage their own content.
      </p>
      <p>
        My Greenwich is free on the App Store:
        <a href={appStore}>apps.apple.com/gb/app/my-greenwich/id6803486290</a>
      </p>
      <p><strong>Ends</strong></p>
    </section>

    <section id="facts">
      <h2>Key facts</h2>
      <dl>
        {facts.map(([k, v]) => (
          <>
            <dt>{k}</dt>
            <dd>{v}</dd>
          </>
        ))}
        <dt>Website</dt>
        <dd><a href="https://mygreenwich.uk">mygreenwich.uk</a></dd>
        <dt>App Store</dt>
        <dd><a href={appStore}>apps.apple.com/gb/app/my-greenwich/id6803486290</a></dd>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section id="notes">
      <h2>Notes to editors</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          My Greenwich is an independent app. It is not produced, funded or endorsed by the
          Royal Borough of Greenwich, UK Parliament or any political party.
        </li>
        <li>
          The app does not show bin collection days. Residents should use the council's
          website for those.
        </li>
        <li>
          Residents do not create an account. The only personal information collected is
          from businesses that choose to submit a listing.
        </li>
        <li>
          Contains public sector information licensed under the Open Government Licence
          v3.0 and Parliamentary information licensed under the Open Parliament Licence
          v3.0.
        </li>
        <li>
          Screenshots, the app icon and a photograph of Philip Brumpton are available on
          request, free to use with coverage of the app.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="about">
      <h2>About Philip Brumpton</h2>
      <p>
        Philip Brumpton is a product designer who has designed for the web since 1999, most
        recently at Which? and before that John Lewis. He lives in Plumstead, where he also
        runs VENDUM Coffee, a small-batch roastery. He designs and builds his own iPhone
        apps. Full background on the <a href="/cv/">CV</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="contact">
      <h2>Press contact</h2>
      <p>
        Philip Brumpton, <a href={`mailto:${pressEmail}`}>{pressEmail}</a>. Available for
        interview, in person in the borough or by phone.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>
